<!-- ── §01 The Filtering Assumption ─────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-assumption" data-code="assumption">
  <div class="section-no">01 — The Filter Assumption</div>
  <h2>98% of the internet gets thrown away</h2>
  <p>
    The dominant pretraining recipe goes like this: start with a raw web crawl (Common Crawl, ~240 trillion tokens before 2023), apply a pipeline of heuristic and model-based filters to keep only "high-quality" text, then train on the remainder. DCLM-Baseline — a recent best-practice dataset — keeps just <strong>2.1%</strong> of the original crawl. RefinedWeb (used by Falcon) keeps 13%. Even loose filters like the English-only filter discard 72% of the data.
  </p>
  <p>
    The intuition is straightforward: junk text (spam, garbled HTML, random characters) should hurt model performance just as bad labels hurt a classifier. So filtering more aggressively should mean better models — or at least never worse.
  </p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">data_retention.py — fraction of CC kept after each filter</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">scroll into view to animate</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <div class="retention-bars" id="retention-bars">
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">CC (unfiltered)</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-cc" data-pct="100"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">100%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">Stop Words filter</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-sw" data-pct="50.4"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">50.4%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">English filter</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-en" data-pct="28.2"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">28.2%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">Repetition filter</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-sw" data-pct="45.3"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">45.3%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">RefinedWeb</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-rw" data-pct="13"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">13%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ret-row">
          <span class="ret-label">DCLM-Baseline</span>
          <div class="ret-track">
            <div class="ret-fill clr-db" data-pct="2.1"></div>
            <span class="ret-pct">2.1%</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size:.72rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.75rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">DCLM-Baseline keeps only 2.1% — ~14M tokens from a 670M-token CC pool used in experiments.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The problem: fewer tokens means less data to learn from at scale. The Chinchilla scaling law prescribes training on roughly 20 tokens per parameter — a 1 trillion parameter model needs 20 trillion tokens. DCLM-Baseline's 3.8 trillion tokens fall short of this budget, even before considering that recent practice is to <em>over-train</em> (more tokens per parameter than Chinchilla-optimal) to produce cheaper-to-serve smaller models.
  </p>
  <p>
    The hypothesis tested by Mohri, Duchi, and Hashimoto (Stanford, 2026) is radical: what if filtering is only useful when you're compute-constrained, and at large enough compute the discarded 97.9% actually helps?
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ── §02 The Experiment ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-experiment" data-code="experiment">
  <div class="section-no">02 — The Experiment</div>
  <h2>Measuring the best possible performance from a dataset</h2>
  <p>
    Standard scaling studies ask: "at a fixed compute budget, which dataset is best?" But this question is misleading — a dataset with more tokens benefits from more training steps, while a filtered dataset with fewer tokens benefits from a larger model (less data → smaller optimal token-to-parameter ratio).
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead, the paper asks: what is the <em>best achievable loss</em> from a dataset, given <em>unlimited compute</em>? Formally:
  </p>
  <div class="math-block">
    <div class="math-label">Objective</div>
    <span class="math-var">L</span>*(D) := min<sub><span class="math-var">M</span>,<span class="math-var">N</span></sub> ℓ(A(D, <span class="math-var">M</span>, <span class="math-var">N</span>))
    <span class="math-comment">  ← best loss over all model sizes M and training steps N</span>
  </div>
  <p>
    This formulation strips away compute as a confound: if a dataset has useful signal, a sufficiently large model trained long enough should extract it. The experiments sweep model sizes from 15M to 7B parameters and training steps across powers of 2, picking the best checkpoint.
  </p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">experiment_setup.py — step-by-step methodology</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">click Next to walk through</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <div class="step-list" id="step-list">
        <div class="step-box active">
          <div class="step-num">1</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <div class="step-title">Sample a CC pool</div>
            <div class="step-desc">Randomly sample m tokens from DCLM-Pool (240T token CC before 2023). Main experiment: m = 670M tokens. Also test 2B and 10B.</div>
            <div class="step-code">Dcc_m = random_sample(CC, m=670_000_000)</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-box">
          <div class="step-num">2</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <div class="step-title">Apply filter variants</div>
            <div class="step-desc">Create 5 filtered versions: English filter (28.2%), Stop Words (50.4%), Repetition (45.3%), RefinedWeb (13%), DCLM-Baseline (2.1%).</div>
            <div class="step-code">D_en  = english_filter(Dcc_m)   # 189M tokens
D_db  = dclm_baseline(Dcc_m)   # 14M tokens</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-box">
          <div class="step-num">3</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <div class="step-title">Sweep model size × training steps</div>
            <div class="step-desc">Train Llama-style models from 15M to 7B params. For each model, vary training steps over powers of 2 (allowing multiple epochs on small datasets).</div>
            <div class="step-code">for M in [15M, 80M, 330M, 1B, 7B]:
  for N in [2^k for k in range(10,28)]:
    model = train(D, M, N)</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step-box">
          <div class="step-num">4</div>
          <div class="step-body">
            <div class="step-title">Compare L*(D) across datasets</div>
            <div class="step-desc">For each dataset, pick the best checkpoint (minimum loss over all M, N). Compare unfiltered CC vs each filter. Evaluate on C4, FineWeb-Edu, Cosmopedia (avg NLL).</div>
            <div class="step-code">L_star = min(loss(train(D, M, N))
              for M, N in all_configs)</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-nav">
        <button id="step-prev" disabled>← Prev</button>
        <button id="step-next">Next →</button>
        <span class="step-prog" id="step-prog">Step 1 of 4</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The experiments use Llama-style dense transformers trained on H200 GPUs with the Meta Lingua codebase, totalling over 20,000 GPU-hours. All runs evaluate on three out-of-distribution datasets: C4, FineWeb-Edu, and Cosmopedia — the reported metric is the average negative log-likelihood across all three.
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ── §03 The Crossover ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-crossover" data-code="crossover">
  <div class="section-no">03 — The Crossover</div>
  <h2>Bigger models flip the ranking</h2>
  <p>
    The main result is a dramatic reversal. At 15M parameters, the unfiltered CC pool is one of the <em>worst</em> performers — the English filter beats it by 0.29 NLL points. At 1B parameters, CC is the <em>best</em> — beating DCLM-Baseline by nearly 2 NLL points and outperforming all five filters.
  </p>

