    <strong>Why does DCLM-Baseline perform so poorly?</strong> At 2.1% retention, the 670M-token pool shrinks to ~14M tokens. By the time total training tokens reach 10<sup>10</sup>, the model has seen each document <em>700+ times</em>. Aggressive epoching on a tiny dataset leads to severe overfitting that no model size can overcome.
  </div>

  <p>
    An important caveat: at 15M–80M parameters, CC never overtakes filtered datasets within a reasonable compute budget. The paper finds that a <strong>sufficiently large model</strong> is required — the 330M threshold is specific to 670M-token pools. With larger pool sizes, the required model size increases (explored in Section 5).
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ── §04 Junk Data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-junk" data-code="junk">
  <div class="section-no">04 — Junk Data is Fine</div>
  <h2>Shuffled words and random strings — models absorb them</h2>
  <p>
    Unfiltered CC still contains real English text, just with more noise. What happens if you inject <em>deliberately constructed</em> junk that contains no useful information?
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper tests two extreme cases:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Randomly generated strings</strong> — vocabulary of 10,000 made-up words (3–8 random lowercase chars), sampled uniformly. Zero linguistic structure.</li>
    <li><strong>Shuffled-word CC documents</strong> — real CC documents with word order randomly permuted. Preserves unigram distribution but destroys syntax, grammar, and most semantics.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">junk_injection.py — best NLL vs injection ratio, 330M vs 1B model</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">hover bars for values</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <canvas id="junk-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;display:block"></canvas>
      <p style="font-size:.7rem;color:var(--muted);margin:.625rem 0 0;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">Bars show best achieved Avg NLL. Lower = better. Left = 330M model. Right = 1B model.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The 330M model results are striking: adding up to +400% shuffled-word documents makes performance <em>worse</em> at first, but the 1B model fully absorbs these datasets — and the +200% and +400% shuffled datasets actually <strong>beat clean CC</strong> at 1B parameters (3.44 vs 3.40).
  </p>

  <p>
    Why do shuffled words help? Even with word order destroyed, "The capital of France is Paris" shuffled to "France capital Paris is the of" still co-locates "France" and "Paris". A large model can extract the unigram-level associations: the additional CC documents (not seen in the original pool) provide new world knowledge, just in scrambled form.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The exception:</strong> shuffled-word documents hurt performance on the <em>first few tokens</em> of a document, because the natural distribution of opening tokens changes. But for any use case involving more than a few tokens of context — which is almost every real application — this effect is negligible.
  </div>

  <p>
    Random strings are harder for models to exploit (no real vocabulary, no co-occurrence signal), but even here the gaps close with model size: at 1B params, +200% random strings matches clean CC (3.40 vs 3.40).
  </p>
</section>

<!-- ── §05 Scaling Laws ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-scaling" data-code="scaling">
  <div class="section-no">05 — Scaling Laws</div>
  <h2>When does the full 240T token CC pool win?</h2>
  <p>
    The 670M-token experiments are proof of concept. The real question: at what compute level does the <em>full</em> 240 trillion token Common Crawl outperform filtered versions at internet scale?
  </p>
  <p>
    The paper scales pool sizes from 670M to 10B tokens and model sizes from 80M to 7B parameters, measuring N*(M, m): the minimum training steps for CC to outperform RefinedWeb. A key finding: N* grows <em>super-linearly</em> with pool size — roughly 1 epoch suffices at 670M tokens, ~3 epochs at 2B tokens, and ~10 epochs at 10B tokens.
  </p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">scaling_law.py — compute needed for CC to beat RefinedWeb</span>
      <span class="vp-hint">log-log scale; 240T target marked</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <canvas id="scaling-canvas" style="width:100%;height:300px;display:block"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    Both scaling law fits (4-epoch constraint and 600 tokens/parameter, following DeepSeek V4) give highly linear log-log relationships (R² &gt; 0.99) and converge near <strong>10<sup>30</sup> FLOPs</strong> for the 240T token CC pool. Current frontier training runs (xAI Grok 4, 2025) are estimated at ~5×10<sup>26</sup> FLOPs — about 10,000× below the threshold.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is a large gap, but not an impossible one: existing forecasts predict 10<sup>29</sup> FLOP training runs by 2030. The authors note this is "far from outlandish" given current compute scaling trends.
  </p>

  <h3>Epoch counts at different pool sizes (1B model)</h3>
  <table class="results-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Pool Size</th><th>Training Epochs at Crossing</th><th>Compute (FLOPs)</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>670M tokens</td><td class="mn">~1.0 epoch</td><td class="mn">~3×10<sup>18</sup></td></tr>
      <tr><td>2B tokens</td><td class="mn">~3.9 epochs</td><td class="mn">~3×10<sup>20</sup></td></tr>
      <tr><td>10B tokens</td><td class="mn">~9.6 epochs</td><td class="mn">~3×10<sup>22</sup></td></tr>
      <tr class="highlight-row"><td>240T tokens (full CC)</td><td class="mn">~4 epochs (projected)</td><td class="mn best">~10<sup>30</sup></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</section>

<!-- ── §06 Theory ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="s-theory" data-code="theory">
  <div class="section-no">06 — Theory</div>
  <h2>Why large models absorb noise: rank separation</h2>
  <p>
    The empirical results raise a natural question: why do large models tolerate (and benefit from) junk data while small models cannot? The paper provides a theoretical explanation via low-rank matrix factorization — the simplest 1-hidden-layer linear neural network.
  </p>

  <div class="vp">
    <div class="vp-head">
      <span class="vp-title">theory.py — rank separation: small vs large model on mixed data</span>
    </div>
    <div class="vp-body">
      <svg id="theory-svg" viewBox="0 0 560 240" style="width:100%;height:auto" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <!-- Left: small model (rank < k, interference) -->
        <text x="140" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" fill="#94a3b8">Small Model (rank &lt; k)</text>
        <text x="140" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">tasks interfere → higher loss</text>

        <!-- Task A blob -->
        <ellipse cx="110" cy="140" rx="55" ry="45" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.15)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,3"/>
        <text x="110" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">Task A</text>
        <text x="110" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">real text</text>
        <text x="110" y="151" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">signals</text>

        <!-- Task B blob (overlapping) -->
        <ellipse cx="170" cy="148" rx="50" ry="42" fill="rgba(239,68,68,0.12)" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-dasharray="4,3"/>
        <text x="177" y="136" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#f87171">Task B</text>
        <text x="177" y="148" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#f87171">noise</text>
        <text x="177" y="159" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#f87171">signals</text>

        <!-- Overlap annotation -->
        <text x="140" y="193" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5" font-family="monospace" fill="#dc2626">✗ overlapping → interference</text>
        <text x="140" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">capacity wasted on conflict</text>
        <text x="140" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">rank r &lt; k tasks</text>

        <!-- Divider -->
        <line x1="280" y1="10" x2="280" y2="235" stroke="#1e293b" stroke-width="1.5"/>

        <!-- Right: large model (rank >= k, separated) -->
        <text x="420" y="22" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-family="monospace" fill="#94a3b8">Large Model (rank ≥ k)</text>
        <text x="420" y="38" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">tasks separated → lower loss</text>

        <!-- Task A blob (separated) -->
        <ellipse cx="365" cy="130" rx="52" ry="42" fill="rgba(139,92,246,0.18)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="365" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">Task A</text>
        <text x="365" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">real text</text>
        <text x="365" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#a78bfa">signals</text>

        <!-- Task B blob (separated, no overlap) -->
        <ellipse cx="472" cy="130" rx="52" ry="42" fill="rgba(16,185,129,0.14)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/>
        <text x="472" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-family="monospace" fill="#34d399">Task B</text>
        <text x="472" y="130" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#34d399">noise</text>
        <text x="472" y="141" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#34d399">signals</text>

        <!-- Gap arrow -->
        <line x1="419" y1="130" x2="421" y2="130" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="1"/>
        <text x="420" y="193" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8.5" font-family="monospace" fill="#10b981">✓ orthogonal subspaces</text>
        <text x="420" y="205" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">no interference between tasks</text>
        <text x="420" y="218" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#64748b">rank r ≥ k tasks</text>

        <!-- Proposition label -->
        <text x="280" y="234" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-family="monospace" fill="#475569">Proposition 7.1: optimal loss = Σ σⱼ² + noise   (sum over j &gt; r)</text>
      </svg>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The formal result (Proposition 7.1) considers k tasks with orthogonal input covariances. A rank-r model achieves optimal loss if and only if r ≥ k — the model has enough capacity to represent every task independently. When r &lt; k, the model is forced to compromise: the singular values of the interaction matrix capture the interference penalty. As model size grows and r exceeds k, the noise tasks are absorbed into orthogonal directions with no penalty to performance on real tasks.
  </p>
  <p>
    The theorem also implies gradient descent is sufficient for optimal fitting when r ≥ k (no non-strict saddle points), which matches the practical finding that standard training finds the optimum.
  </p>
  <p>
    A practical implication: the "junk capacity" scales with model size. A 1B parameter model has ~10× the rank capacity of a 100M parameter model. As long as the real-data tasks occupy a lower-dimensional subspace than the total rank, additional noise tasks are absorbed for free. This aligns with the empirical observation that model size, not training steps alone, is the key lever for tolerating unfiltered data.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The Bitter Lesson connection:</strong> Sutton's original bitter lesson (2019) argued that methods leveraging <em>compute</em> consistently outperform methods leveraging <em>human knowledge</em>. This paper applies the same pattern to data curation: hand-designed quality filters that look useful at small scale are eventually replaced by raw scale. The compute invested in a better filter is better spent on a larger model.
  </div>
</section>

</article>

